[39;49m[K[39m[49m[0m
[39;49m[K[2m• [22mComputer terminals began as electromechanical teleprinters that let operators[39m[49m[0m
[39;49m[K  send commands to distant mainframe computers and receive printed output. In[39m[49m[0m
[39;49m[K  the 1960s and 1970s, video display terminals replaced paper output with[39m[49m[0m
[39;49m[K  screens, making interactive computing faster and more practical. Devices like[39m[49m[0m
[39;49m[K  the DEC VT100 helped standardize terminal behavior, including cursor control[39m[49m[0m
[39;49m[K  and escape sequences still echoed in modern terminal emulators. As personal[39m[49m[0m
[39;49m[K  computers and graphical interfaces spread, physical terminals became less[39m[49m[0m
[39;49m[K  common, but their command-line model remained central to software development[39m[49m[0m
[39;49m[K  and system administration. Today, terminal emulators preserve that history by[39m[49m[0m
